      "id": 1,
      "text": "Kingsford was founded when Henry Ford built a wooden car factory in 1923, later renamed after developer Edward G.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        7,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.ironmountain.org/pages/history-of-the-area/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Ford plant produced wooden station wagon bodies starting in 1931, and later built 4,190 Waco CG‑4 gliders for WWII.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingsford%2C_Michigan"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A locally built WWII glider, now housed at the World War II Glider and Military Museum, is one of only seven fully restored CG‑4A models.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.ironmountain.org/pages/history-of-the-area/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Kingsford sits in Dickinson County, which was organized in 1891 from parts of Marquette, Menominee, and Iron counties.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
